    <statement><p>Find the linear approximation to <m>f(x,y,z)=\frac{xy}{z}</m> at the point <m>(1,3,2)</m>:</p>
<p><m>f(x,y,z)\approx</m><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>1.5x+0.5y-0.75z</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(1,3,2)</m>:

<me>\left. \begin{array}{lcl}
f(x,y,z)=\frac{xy}{z} \amp \amp  f(1,3,2)=1.5 \\
f_x(x,y,z)=\frac{y}{z} \amp  \Rightarrow \amp  f_x(1,3,2)=1.5 \\
f_y(x,y,z)=\frac{x}{z}  \amp \amp  f_y(1,3,2)=0.5 \\
f_z(x,y,z)=-\frac{xy}{z^{2}} \amp \amp  f_z(1,3,2)=-0.75
\end{array} \right.</me>

The linear approximation is therefore
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx f(1,3,2)+f_x(1,3,2)(x-1)+</me><me>f_y(1,3,2)(y-3)+f_z(1,3,2)(z-2)</me>
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx 1.5+1.5\mathopen{}\left(x-1\right)+0.5\mathopen{}\left(y-3\right)-0.75\mathopen{}\left(z-2\right)</me>
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx 1.5x+0.5y-0.75z</me>





</solution>
